    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-cdb07d9d2470\",\"class\":\"expressible\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"config-env-dot-not-allowlisted\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"pattern\":\"(?<![\\\\w$])process\\\\.env\\\\.(?!(?:PORT|DATABASE_URL|LOG_LEVEL|SMTP_HOST|SMTP_FROM|INVOICE_PREFIX)(?![\\\\w$]))[A-Za-z_$]\",\"path\":\"src/config/**\"},{\"id\":\"config-env-bracket\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"pattern\":\"(?<![\\\\w$])process\\\\.env\\\\[\",\"path\":\"src/config/**\"}],\"coverage\":\"Every line of every src/config TypeScript file, comments included. The first constraint flags process.env.NAME where NAME is not exactly one of PORT, DATABASE_URL, LOG_LEVEL, SMTP_HOST, SMTP_FROM or INVOICE_PREFIX; longer names such as PORTX or PORT_X are flagged. The second flags any process.env[...] access. Destructuring and process.env?.NAME are not matched, as the rule says.\",\"residual\":null,\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/config/extra.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+// reads process.env.SECRET_KEY\\n+export const secret = process.env.SECRET_KEY;\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/config/extra2.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const port = process.env[\\\"PORT\\\"];\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/config/ok.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\\n+export const port = process.env.PORT;\\n+export const db = process.env.DATABASE_URL;\\n+const { SECRET } = process.env;\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"The rule is purely lexical. It applies within src/config, comments included, so it reduces to two line patterns: dotted access with a name outside the allowlist (an identifier-boundary lookahead stops prefixes like PORTX from passing), and bracket access. Forms the rule leaves uncovered, destructuring and optional chaining, are deliberately not matched. Nothing needs meaning or intent.\"}",
